rt_retries": 0, "rate_limit_refusals": 0, "provider_requests_by_model": {"openai:gpt-6-luna": 13}, "reserved_worst_case_usd": 0.031067, "reservation_was_sufficient": true, "llm_failed_calls": 1, "input_tokens": 25159, "output_tokens": 11247, "calls_by_provider": {"openai": 13}, "calls_by_role": {"planner": 3, "researcher": 7, "critic": 2, "synthesizer": 1}, "known_cost_usd": 0.008139, "cost_is_complete": false, "unpriced_calls": 0, "unpriced_categories": 8, "cached_input_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 23150, "reasoning_tokens": 8068, "citation_integrity_rate": 1.0, "evidence_integrity_rate": null, "citation_coverage_rate": 1.0, "claim_support_rate": 0.0, "partial_support_rate": 0.0, "support_breakdown": {"supported": 0, "partially_supported": 0, "unsupported": 3, "not_checked": 0}, "entailment_exhaustive": true, "citations_total": 0, "evidence_refs_total": 0, "contradictions_total": 0, "contradictions_auditable": 0, "unused_sources": 6, "citations_repaired": false, "stage_seconds": {"analyze_query": 12.497, "plan_research": 5.355, "generate_queries": 5.486, "dedupe_sources": 0.003, "register_sources": 0.0, "assess_coverage": 9.888, "synthesize": 42.73, "verify_citations": 11.873}, "errors": 0, "error_kinds": {}}, "markdown": "# Large Language Models and Neural Networks: What the Evidence Establishes\n\n**Question:** How does a large language model differ from a neural network?\n\n## Source excerpts\n\n_No synthesized claim passed evidence verification. Showing exact source excerpts instead. These are verbatim quotations, not findings, and no conclusion has been drawn from them._\n\n- \"A neural network is a machine learning model that stacks simple \"neurons\" in layers and learns pattern-recognizing weights and biases from data to map inputs to outputs.\" \u2014 **[S1]** What Is a Neural Network? | IBM\n- \"They underpin breakthroughs in computer vision, natural language processing (NLP), speech recognition\u00a0and countless real-world applications ranging from forecasting to facial recognition.\" \u2014 **[S1]** What Is a Neural Network? | IBM\n- \"Generative AI builds on the same principles at greater scale. Large language models, diffusion models, VAEs and GANs learn distributions over data to synthesize text, images, audio and code.\" \u2014 **[S1]** What Is a Neural Network? | IBM\n- \"Generative AI builds on the same principles at greater scale. Large language models, diffusion models, VAEs and GANs learn distributions over data to synthesize text, images, audio and code.\" \u2014 **[S1]** What Is a Neural Network? | IBM\n- \"Modern neural network architectures\u2014such as transformers and encoder-decoder models\u2014follow the same core principles (learned weights and biases, stacked layers, nonlinear activations, end-to-end training by backpropagation).\" \u2014 **[S1]** What Is a Neural Network? | IBM\n- \"Instead of fully connected mixing alone, transformers use attention to form data-dependent weighted combinations of representations, alongside residual connections, normalization and positional encodings to enrich wiring built on the same fundamentals.\" \u2014 **[S1]** What Is a Neural Network? | IBM\n- \"A neural network is a computer system that tries to imitate how the human brain works. It consists of many artificial neurons connected to each other and can process information by learning from data. A neural network can perform tasks such as speech recognition, image analysis, and adaptive control.\" \u2014 **[S2]** What are Neural Networks? \u2014 updated 2026 | IxDF\n- \"For example, OpenAI developed GPT, a large-scale generative pre-trained transformer model that can generate coherent and diverse text on various topics in response to AI prompts.\" \u2014 **[S2]** What are Neural Networks? \u2014 updated 2026 | IxDF\n- \"Natural language processing: Neural networks can analyze and generate natural language texts for various purposes, such as sentiment analysis, question answering, text summarization, and text generation. For example, OpenAI developed GPT, a large-scale generative pre-trained transformer model that can generate coherent and diverse text on various topics in response to AI prompts.\" \u2014 **[S2]** What are Neural Networks? \u2014 updated 2026 | IxDF\n- \"Neural networks can also have different architectures, such as feedforward, recurrent, convolutional, or generative adversarial networks.\" \u2014 **[S2]** What are Neural Networks? \u2014 updated 2026 | IxDF\n- \"For example, OpenAI developed GPT, a large-scale generative pre-trained transformer model that can generate coherent and diverse text on various topics in response to AI prompts.\" \u2014 **[S2]** What are Neural Networks? \u2014 updated 2026 | IxDF\n- \"Neural networks can recognize and classify objects, faces, scenes, and activities in images.\" \u2014 **[S2]** What are Neural Networks? \u2014 updated 2026 | IxDF\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The evidence does not directly establish whether large language models are a kind of neural network, whether all large language models use neural-network architectures, or whether either category includes models outside the other.\n- The evidence does not provide an explicit, fully supported contrast between the terms; it separately describes neural networks by their layered input-to-output structure and LLMs by generative behavior.\n- The evidence does not establish a specific definition of an LLM based on language-focused training data, training objectives, or a typical scale threshold.\n- Only limited evidence was found for: What makes a model a large language model, including its language focus, training data and objectives, and typical scale.\n- Only limited evidence was found for: How do LLMs relate taxonomically to neural networks, and are there LLMs or neural networks that fall outside the other category.\n- Only limited evidence was found for: What common misconceptions blur the distinction between the terms, such as treating an LLM as a synonym for any neural network or assuming all neural networks process language.\n- 3 generated claim(s) were excluded because the cited evidence did not support them, or because verification did not reach them within this run's budget.\n- This research did not answer the question. No claim survived verification, and the material below is background rather than an answer.\n- The evidence did not establish a named dimension along which they differ.\n- The evidence did not establish how the subjects relate, such as one being a kind of the other.\n\n## Sources\n\n- **[S1]** [What Is a Neural Network? | IBM](https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/neural-networks) \u2014 ibm.com, other, n.d., quality 0.64 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S2]** [What are Neural Networks? \u2014 updated 2026 | IxDF](https://ixdf.org/literature/topics/neural-networks) \u2014 ixdf.org, other, n.d., quality 0.64 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S3]** [Types of Neural Networks - GeeksforGeeks](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/deep-learning/types-of-neural-networks) \u2014 geeksforgeeks.org, other, n.d., quality 0.64 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S4]** [Neural network (machine learning)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neural_network_(machine_learning)) \u2014 en.wikipedia.org, other, n.d., quality 0.64 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S5]** [Neural Network Examples, Applications, and Use Cases | Coursera](https://www.coursera.org/articles/neural-network-example) \u2014 coursera.org, other, n.d., quality 0.64 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S6]** [Neural Networks in Machine Vision: How They Work & Applications | Basler AG](https://www.baslerweb.com/en/learning/neural-networks) \u2014 baslerweb.com, other, n.d., quality 0.64 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n\n## Citation verification\n\n- Evidence references: 0, 0 resolved to citable evidence (n/a, no references). Citation markers are derived from those references by the engine, so citation integrity is a structural invariant rather than a measurement.\n- Evidence-owing claims